It matters, but probably not in the simple “same atoms = same self” sense.

Matter is the carrier. Organization, causal continuity, and dynamics may be the more important invariants. So the stronger statement is:

matter matters because the pattern must be instantiated somewhere; the exact matter may not matter if the relevant organization survives.

That is why your screw example is useful. The screw has more material continuity than much of your body, but far less identity-bearing structure.

So:

matter ≠ identity
matter enables identity
continuity may live more in process than in substance

And yes, that absolutely matters, because if true in the right sense, it opens the door conceptually to substrate transfer: preserving an operator or process while changing what physically carries it.

The unresolved part is consciousness. We know patterns and functions can transfer across substrates in many domains. We do not know yet whether first-person conscious continuity can.

So: matter matters. Just maybe not as much as the pattern moving through it.